1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:01,955
Game ten.

2
00:00:02,075 --> 00:00:06,521
You are White, fifteen minutes plus ten seconds, and you win in forty moves.

3
00:00:06,641 --> 00:00:13,317
Before move one you decided to meet the Sicilian the open way, and you recorded yourself thinking out loud until move thirty one.

4
00:00:13,437 --> 00:00:14,366
The Najdorf.

5
00:00:14,486 --> 00:00:15,705
Three acts to come.

6
00:00:15,825 --> 00:00:18,669
A planning question you asked out loud at move eight.

7
00:00:18,789 --> 00:00:21,599
A trick you saw coming and still counted one move short.

8
00:00:21,719 --> 00:00:25,039
And a rook ending you converted with a plan you also said out loud.

9
00:00:25,859 --> 00:00:31,862
You weighed bishop to c4 against bishop to g5, and chose g5 so that you could always take the knight.

10
00:00:31,982 --> 00:00:33,456
The engine ranks it first.

11
00:00:33,576 --> 00:00:37,164
Pawn to h6 asks the bishop the question one move too early.

12
00:00:37,284 --> 00:00:43,240
The pawn on e7 still blocks the queen, so if you take on f6, Black has to recapture with a pawn.

13
00:00:44,060 --> 00:00:46,486
You retreated, naturally, as you put it.

14
00:00:46,606 --> 00:00:48,777
Taking was worth more than half a pawn.

15
00:00:48,897 --> 00:00:58,011
After the pawn recaptures, Black's king has no safe home, d5 and f5 are holes, and Black must spend pawn to h5 just to keep your queen out.

16
00:00:58,131 --> 00:01:00,534
The h6 move becomes a lost tempo.

17
00:01:00,654 --> 00:01:05,960
When a pawn questions your pinning bishop, check whether taking wrecks the structure before you retreat.

18
00:01:08,280 --> 00:01:10,277
Here is the quiet moment of the game.

19
00:01:10,397 --> 00:01:15,203
Nothing is hanging and there is no tactic, only a question, and you asked it out loud.

20
00:01:15,323 --> 00:01:17,262
Bishop to c4 or to e2.

21
00:01:17,382 --> 00:01:18,834
Castle short or long.

22
00:01:18,954 --> 00:01:20,800
Pawn to f4 now, or later.

23
00:01:20,920 --> 00:01:28,652
At one common depth, bishop to e2, queen to e2, queen to f3 and pawn to a3 all sit within about a tenth of a pawn.

24
00:01:28,772 --> 00:01:31,059
Pawn to f4 stands half a pawn clear.

25
00:01:31,179 --> 00:01:34,174
So among the quiet moves the choice did not matter.

26
00:01:34,294 --> 00:01:37,336
The plan did, and it starts with the pawn you postponed.

27
00:01:39,456 --> 00:01:40,304
Your plan:

28
00:01:40,424 --> 00:01:41,515
f4 at once.

29
00:01:41,635 --> 00:01:46,604
The bishop comes home from h4 to f2, where it guards d4 against the queen raid.

30
00:01:46,724 --> 00:01:50,114
The queen goes to f3 to hold e4, and you castle long.

31
00:01:50,234 --> 00:01:51,024
Two rules.

32
00:01:51,144 --> 00:01:53,942
The pawn on e4 always has a second defender.

33
00:01:54,062 --> 00:01:56,070
The bishop never waits on h4.

34
00:01:56,190 --> 00:01:58,733
Black's plan is the classic Najdorf one.

35
00:01:58,853 --> 00:02:03,613
Pawn to b5 and b4 chases the c3 knight, the only defender of e4.

36
00:02:03,733 --> 00:02:06,566
Pawn to g5 chases your bishop off h4.

37
00:02:06,686 --> 00:02:09,159
Both plans are about the same two things:

38
00:02:09,279 --> 00:02:12,181
the pawn on e4, and the loose bishop on h4.

39
00:02:12,301 --> 00:02:17,212
You chose bishop to e2, then queen to d2, and that is where the fault line opened.

40
00:02:17,332 --> 00:02:25,726
Your e4 pawn had one defender, the h4 bishop had none, and the only thing standing between it and the e7 bishop was the knight on f6.

41
00:02:25,846 --> 00:02:32,069
In the engine's plan, knight takes e4 simply loses a piece, because the queen on f3 covers the square.

42
00:02:34,389 --> 00:02:36,723
Right after playing queen to d2 you said it:

43
00:02:36,843 --> 00:02:38,956
that is bad, the knight takes e4.

44
00:02:39,076 --> 00:02:40,063
And it came.

45
00:02:40,183 --> 00:02:45,070
The knight hits your queen and your knight, and it unmasks the e7 bishop onto yours.

46
00:02:45,190 --> 00:02:47,303
But the unmasked bishop is loose too.

47
00:02:47,423 --> 00:02:49,316
You looked at exactly this line.

48
00:02:49,436 --> 00:02:53,639
Bishop takes, knight takes, bishop takes, and you called it a mass trade.

49
00:02:53,759 --> 00:02:57,880
One move further, Black's knight is still on d2 and your bishop is on d8.

50
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:01,855
Both are hanging, Black is to move, and only one can be saved.

51
00:03:01,975 --> 00:03:03,844
White comes out a clean piece up.

52
00:03:05,964 --> 00:03:09,157
Instead the knight took on e4 and the bishop took on h4.

53
00:03:09,277 --> 00:03:11,285
Let's call it an attack, you said.

54
00:03:11,405 --> 00:03:13,065
The engine sees no attack:

55
00:03:13,185 --> 00:03:17,876
Black has the pawn and the bishop pair, and the bishop on h4 is already eyeing f2.

56
00:03:18,696 --> 00:03:22,608
A pawn down, your compensation was one weak pawn, on d6.

57
00:03:22,728 --> 00:03:26,699
You asked whether trades would win it back, and the instinct was right.

58
00:03:26,819 --> 00:03:29,280
But first stop the pawn from advancing.

59
00:03:29,400 --> 00:03:34,114
Pawn to c4 was on offer twice, and it keeps pawn to d5 from being free.

60
00:03:34,934 --> 00:03:36,490
Then Black handed it back.

61
00:03:36,610 --> 00:03:39,106
Until now d6 was safe for one reason:

62
00:03:39,226 --> 00:03:43,661
your own knight on d4 blocked the file, so the queen and rook could not reach it.

63
00:03:43,781 --> 00:03:46,440
Knight to c6 offers to trade off that plug.

64
00:03:46,560 --> 00:03:52,678
Knight takes, pawn takes, and knight takes d6 with check, so Black never gets to add a defender.

65
00:03:52,798 --> 00:03:55,805
The pawn is back, and Black's queenside is in pieces.

66
00:03:58,425 --> 00:04:01,269
Queens off, a pawn up, and you had a plan:

67
00:04:01,389 --> 00:04:05,674
bishop to f3 against c6, then doubled rooks on the seventh rank.

68
00:04:05,794 --> 00:04:06,896
The plan is fine.

69
00:04:07,016 --> 00:04:08,096
The order is not.

70
00:04:08,216 --> 00:04:15,426
King to e7 hits the rook on d6, and now bishop takes c6 is no longer check, so the king simply takes the rook.

71
00:04:15,546 --> 00:04:19,052
The king also covers d7, which shuts your seventh rank.

72
00:04:19,172 --> 00:04:20,960
The engine gives under one pawn.

73
00:04:22,580 --> 00:04:24,844
Rook to d1 first defends d6.

74
00:04:24,964 --> 00:04:29,515
Then the same king move runs into a check on d7, and the bishop on b7 falls.

75
00:04:30,335 --> 00:04:33,911
Black played rook to d8 instead, and you saw what it allowed.

76
00:04:34,031 --> 00:04:38,617
You worked out bishop takes c6 with check at the board, and it wins a clean pawn.

77
00:04:38,737 --> 00:04:41,837
The rook ending that follows is the real test of the game.

78
00:04:41,957 --> 00:04:45,289
You are a pawn up, with three pawns against one on the queenside.

79
00:04:49,109 --> 00:04:51,396
At move twenty three you weighed two plans:

80
00:04:51,516 --> 00:04:55,463
walk the king over to collect the a-pawn, or roll the c-pawn at once.

81
00:04:55,583 --> 00:04:59,763
Pawn to b3 serves both, an escape square that also prepares c4.

82
00:04:59,883 --> 00:05:02,542
You hesitated between king to b2 and c4.

83
00:05:02,662 --> 00:05:05,077
The engine scores them exactly the same.

84
00:05:05,797 --> 00:05:11,613
Once the rooks came off, you pushed c4 to put on the pressure, and that was the engine's first choice.

85
00:05:12,433 --> 00:05:14,778
Then came your clearest decision of the game.

86
00:05:14,898 --> 00:05:16,698
The simple plan, you said:

87
00:05:16,818 --> 00:05:19,825
a3, b4, and push both pawns.

88
00:05:19,945 --> 00:05:22,720
Push c5 at once, and the lone pawn falls.

89
00:05:22,840 --> 00:05:29,283
The rook attacks it from c8, b4 cannot support it while the a-pawn can take, and the king drives your rook away.

90
00:05:29,403 --> 00:05:30,460
The win is gone.

91
00:05:32,680 --> 00:05:37,080
With a3 first, the trade on b4 leaves you two connected passed pawns.

92
00:05:37,900 --> 00:05:40,245
Black's king went raiding on the other wing.

93
00:05:40,365 --> 00:05:43,720
Your rook retreated to d1, to avoid complications.

94
00:05:43,840 --> 00:05:47,962
On d2 it would still guard f2, and the raid would find nothing to take.

95
00:05:48,082 --> 00:05:52,656
When you finally pushed c5, you called yourself an idiot for not doing it earlier.

96
00:05:52,776 --> 00:05:57,838
Before a3 it would have thrown the win away, and a move later it was worth a fifth of a pawn.

97
00:05:57,958 --> 00:06:00,118
A move-order detail, not a mistake.

98
00:06:00,238 --> 00:06:02,734
It did fall, but the raid is too slow.

99
00:06:02,854 --> 00:06:06,221
Every king move on the kingside is a free push for your c-pawn.

100
00:06:07,641 --> 00:06:08,790
The final race.

101
00:06:08,910 --> 00:06:12,416
Black is lost after any move here, and the race shows why:

102
00:06:12,536 --> 00:06:16,495
the e-pawn runs, your b-pawn runs, and Black is one tempo short.

103
00:06:19,615 --> 00:06:24,480
Pawn to e2 is one step from queening, but your rook needs only one move to cover e1.

104
00:06:24,600 --> 00:06:28,663
Rook takes f7 with check, then rook to e7, behind the pawn.

105
00:06:28,783 --> 00:06:31,640
Pawn to b8 is next, and Black resigns.

106
00:06:32,460 --> 00:06:33,562
One lesson to keep.

107
00:06:33,682 --> 00:06:42,007
When the position asks a planning question, the answer is usually the move that shores up your weak points now, not the one that keeps every option open.

108
00:06:42,127 --> 00:06:44,031
At move eight that was f4.

109
00:06:44,151 --> 00:06:47,146
The rook ending, you planned out loud and played well.

